An **export expression** tells NAHPU what to write: which record fields to use,
what text to put around them, and what to do when a value is missing. You write
expressions in two places:

- the text elements of a [document template](../export-documents), such as a
  specimen label;
- the `Custom field` columns of a [tabular export preset](../export-records).

The same expression gives the same result in both. For example,
`[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet]` {% ref id="C" /%} prints
`Rattus rattus` on a label and writes `Rattus rattus` in a spreadsheet column.

## The basics in one minute

A field goes in square brackets, written as `[table::field]`. Everything outside
brackets is written as it is:

```text
Field no. [specimen::fieldNumber]
```

prints `Field no. 156`. Three small additions cover most needs:

| Add | To | Example | Prints |
|---|---|---|---|
| `??text` | Write `text` when the field is empty | `[taxonomy::specificEpithet??sp.]` | `sp.` when no epithet is recorded |
| `#label` | Write a coded field's label instead of its code | `[mammalAttribute::testisPosition#label]` | `Scrotal` instead of `0` |
| `[[if]…]]` | Write text only when a condition holds | `[[if][coordinate::elevationInMeter!=""]=>"m"]]` | `m` only when an elevation is recorded |

You rarely type keys by hand. `Insert Field` in the template editor and
`Add field` in a custom field insert them for you.

## Common expressions {% #common-expressions %}

Copy these into a text element of a document template, or into a tabular
column as described with each one. They use specimen fields.

### Species name {% #species-name %}

```text
[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet]
```

Prints `Rattus rattus`. For specimens identified only to genus, add a fallback
so the name reads `Rattus sp.`:

```text
[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet??sp.]
```

For the full name, including a subspecies, use `[specimen::scientificName]`.
In a tabular preset, put either expression in a `Custom field` column.

### Full locality {% #full-locality %}

Joins the geography fields into one value and skips the empty ones, so a
missing county does not leave a doubled comma:

```text
[geography::country]|[geography::islandGroup]|[geography::stateProvince]|[geography::county]|[geography::municipality]|[geography::locality]
```

Each `|` separates two parts. The separator that is actually written, such as a
comma, comes from the list settings:

- **Document template:** set `Text type` to `List Values` and choose the comma
  format.
- **Tabular export preset:** set `Mapping type` to `List field`, `List output`
  to `One column`, and `Separator` to `Comma (A, B)`. Paste the expression into
  `Advanced` > `Raw source expression` and fill in `Column name (optional)`;
  the Darwin Core and NAHPU namespace header formats require a name.

A record prints `Indonesia, West Java, Bogor, Cibodas`; one without a county
prints `Indonesia, West Java, Cibodas`. Remove, reorder, or add parts as
needed.

### Testis size {% #testis-size %}

Prints `length x width` only when the testis is scrotal and both measurements
are recorded. Every other record prints nothing:

```text
[[if][mammalAttribute::testisPosition=="0"&&mammalAttribute::testisLength!=""&&mammalAttribute::testisWidth!=""]=>"[mammalAttribute::testisLength] x [mammalAttribute::testisWidth]"]]
```

| Record | Output |
|---|---|
| Scrotal, 10 mm by 6.5 mm | `10 x 6.5` |
| Scrotal, width not recorded | Nothing |
| Abdominal | Nothing |
| Female | Nothing |

`"0"` is the stored code for Scrotal. Add ` mm` inside the quotes for a unit, or
`|"not scrotal"` before the closing `]]` to write something for every other
record. See [Conditional text](#conditional-text) for how this works. In a
tabular preset, use a `Custom field` column.

### Encoded field labels {% #encoded-labels %}

```text
[mammalAttribute::testisPosition#label]
```

Prints `Scrotal` instead of the stored code `0`. It works in any text type,
including a `Custom field` column. See
[Decode an encoded field](#decode-an-encoded-field).

## Placeholders in detail

### Symbols

| Symbol | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `[ ]` | A field placeholder. | `[fieldNumber]` |
| `::` | Separates table from field in a full key. | `[specimen::fieldNumber]` |
| `??` | Fallback written when the field is empty. | `[specimen::preparator??N/A]` |
| `#label` | Writes a coded field's label instead of its code. | `[mammalAttribute::testisPosition#label]` |
| `-img` | Draws a `.sex` field as an icon instead of text. | `[mammal.sex]-img` |
| `[[ ]]` | A conditional expression. | `[[specimen::condition][…]]` |
| `[[if]` … `]]` | Conditional text, written only when the conditions match. | `[[if][…]=>" mm"]]` |

### Field keys

A placeholder holds a full `table::field` key or just the field name:

```text
Field number: [specimen::fieldNumber]
Scientific name: [taxonomy::scientificName]
Locality: [site::specificLocality]
```

A short form such as `[fieldNumber]` also works, but use the full key when two
tables have a field with the same name. The field picker only offers fields the
template's or preset's record type can supply.

### Missing values

`??` sets what to write when a field is empty. The `No Content Placeholder`
control writes it for you:

| Option | Written as | Output when the field is empty |
|---|---|---|
| `Blank` | `[specimen::preparator]` | Nothing |
| `table::field` | `[specimen::preparator??specimen::preparator]` | The field key, as a visible reminder |
| `N/A` | `[specimen::preparator??N/A]` | `N/A` |
| `None` | `[specimen::preparator??None]` | `None` |
| Custom | `[specimen::preparator??not recorded]` | Whatever you typed |

The text after `??` is written as it is. A placeholder ending in `-img` cannot
take a fallback.

### Sex icons and pictures

Two placeholders draw an image instead of text. Each must be alone in its text
element:

- `[mammal.sex]-img` draws the sex as an icon. The key must end in `.sex`. Set
  the icon size with the width and height fields.
- `[media::media]`, with `Text type` set to `Picture`, draws the record's
  attached images in a grid.

## Custom fields

A custom field mixes fields with your own text, or holds text alone. In a
document template, every text element is already a custom field: insert fields
with `Insert Field` and type around them. In a tabular export preset, select
`Add custom field` in `Edit Fields`, or set an existing column's
`Mapping type` to `Custom field`.

The custom field editor builds the expression one piece, or segment, at a
time:

| Control | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Add field` | Adds a field chosen from the field picker. |
| `Add text` {% ref id="B" /%} | Adds text or a separator. |
| `Add conditional text` | Adds [conditional text](#conditional-text): text written only when its conditions match. |
| `Show label` | On a coded field, adds `#label` so the column shows the label instead of the code. |
| `Edit conditional text` | Reopens a conditional text segment. |
| `Move segment up` / `Move segment down` | Reorders segments. They are written left to right. |
| `Remove segment` | Deletes a segment. |
| `Expression:` | Shows the expression that will be saved. |
| `Column name` | Names the output column. |

Each of these is a complete custom field:

```text
[personnel::initial]-[specimen::fieldNumber]
[taxonomy::genus] [taxonomy::specificEpithet]
NAHPU
```

The last one writes `NAHPU` in every row, which suits a fixed column such as an
institution code. A custom field with a `Column name` and no segments at all
writes an empty column, for a receiving sheet's column NAHPU does not record.

Give every custom field a `Column name`. It is required with the Darwin Core and
NAHPU namespace header formats. With `table::fieldName` or `fieldName`, an
unnamed custom field is headed by its first field, or by its text when it has no
field.

A custom field column writes its expression as plain text. For a value format
such as `Encoded text` or `Date`, for conditional brackets, or for indexed list
columns, use a `Single field` or `List field` column instead.

## Conditional output

A conditional expression checks one or more fields and then changes what is
written. Build one with `Conditional output` in the template editor's text
toolbar, which writes the syntax for you and reopens it for editing. In a
tabular preset, choose a conditional `Value format` on a `Single field` column;
its own field is the target.

### Choose a type

| You want to… | Type | Example result |
|---|---|---|
| Put a value in brackets when another field says so | `Conditional brackets` | `[82.5]` when the weight was estimated |
| Replace a value with your own text when another field says so | `Conditional field` | `?` instead of the epithet when the ID is uncertain |
| Replace a value based on the value itself | `Conditional value` | `Not recorded` when the value is `0` |
| Write text that has no field of its own, such as a unit | `Conditional text` | ` mm` only after a recorded width |

In the dialog:

{% steps %}

1. Choose the `Conditional type`.
2. Choose the `Target field`, the value that is bracketed or replaced.
   `Conditional text` has no target; it is added to the end of the element's
   text.
3. Add conditions. Each one has a `Controlling field`, a comparison, and a
   value. `Conditional value` uses the target itself, so its field picker is
   hidden.
4. With more than one condition, set `Match logic` to
   `Any condition (OR)` or `All conditions (AND)`.
5. Fill in `Replacement text`, or `Text when matched` and
   `Text otherwise (optional)` for conditional text. Leave `Replacement text`
   empty to hide the value when the conditions match.

{% /steps %}

### Conditional text (if and else) {% #conditional-text %}

Conditional text writes text depending on the conditions, instead of changing
a field. Use it for text that belongs with a value but should appear only with
it, such as a unit:

```text
[mammalAttribute::testisWidth][[if][mammalAttribute::testisWidth!=""]=>" mm"]]
```

A width of `6.5` prints `6.5 mm`; a missing width prints nothing, not a stray
`mm`.

Add a second text after `|` to write something when the conditions do not
match:

```text
[[if][mammalAttribute::testisPosition=="0"]=>"scrotal"|"not scrotal"]]
```

The text can contain fields, with fallbacks and `#label`. They are filled in
when the text is written:

```text
[[if][mammalAttribute::testisLength!=""&&mammalAttribute::testisWidth!=""]=>"[mammalAttribute::testisPosition#label], [mammalAttribute::testisLength] x [mammalAttribute::testisWidth] mm"]]
```

- Put each text in quotes. Write a quote inside it as `\"`. A `|` inside the
  quotes is ordinary text.
- Without a second text, nothing is written when the conditions do not match.
- A conditional expression inside the text is not evaluated.
- `if` must be lowercase.

### Hide a value unless another field is recorded

Use `Is empty` with an empty replacement. This prints the testis length only
when a testis position was recorded:

```text
[[mammalAttribute::testisLength][mammalAttribute::testisPosition==""]=>""]]
```

A conditional hides only its own field. Text beside it, such as a unit or the
` x ` between two measurements, still prints. Give that text its own condition
with conditional text:

```text
[[mammalAttribute::testisLength][mammalAttribute::testisPosition==""]=>""]][[if][mammalAttribute::testisPosition!=""&&mammalAttribute::testisLength!=""]=>" mm"]]
```

[Testis size](#testis-size) shows the complete pattern.

### Worked examples

**Bracket an estimated weight.** A mammal's accuracy note names the
measurements that were not measured precisely. Bracket the weight when it is
one of them:

```text
[[mammalAttribute::weight][mammalAttribute::accuracy~="weight"]]
```

A flagged weight prints `[82.5]`; a precise one prints `82.5`. On
`mammalAttribute::accuracy`, `~=` asks whether the named measurement is
flagged, not whether the text contains the word.

**Mark an uncertain identification.** ID confidence is stored as `0` (Low),
`1` (Medium), or `2` (High), so "not High" is `!="2"`. Put the genus and the
epithet in one text element and make the epithet conditional:

```text
[taxonomy::genus] [[taxonomy::specificEpithet][specimen::iDConfidence!="2"]=>"?"]]
```

A high-confidence identification prints `Rattus rattus`; anything less prints
`Rattus ?`. The replacement takes the place of the epithet; it is not added
after it. To keep a value and add something after it, write the value and
follow it with [conditional text](#conditional-text).

`!="2"` does not match a record with no confidence recorded. To include those
too, add `Is empty` as an extra OR condition:

```text
[[taxonomy::specificEpithet][specimen::iDConfidence=="0"||specimen::iDConfidence=="1"||specimen::iDConfidence==""]=>"?"]]
```

### Rules for conditions

- **Conditions compare stored values, not labels.** A coded field is compared
  by its code: write `specimen::iDConfidence!="2"`, not `!="High"`, and
  `mammalAttribute::testisPosition=="0"`, not `=="Scrotal"`.
- **Comparisons are case-sensitive** and ignore spaces at either end.
- **A blank field never matches** an ordinary comparison, even `!=`. Use
  `Is empty` (`==""`) or `Is not empty` (`!=""`) to test for a value.
- **Use one joiner per expression:** all `&&` or all `||`, not both.
- **An empty target** uses its `No Content Placeholder`, not the replacement.
- **A replacement replaces the whole value.** An empty replacement hides it.
- **A mistyped expression prints as plain text.** Preview a record that matches
  and one that does not.

### Syntax reference

The dialog writes these for you; use this table to read or edit an expression
by hand.

| Symbol | Name | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `[[` … `]]` | Expression delimiters | Open and close a conditional expression. |
| `][` | Target separator | Ends the target field and starts the conditions. |
| `==` | Equals | Matches when the value is exactly the comparison value. |
| `!=` | Not equals | Matches when the value differs from the comparison value. |
| `~=` | Contains | Matches when the comparison value occurs anywhere in the value. |
| `!=""` | Is not empty | Matches when the field holds any value. |
| `==""` | Is empty | Matches when the field is blank or absent. |
| `&&` | All | Every condition must match. |
| `\|\|` | Any | At least one condition must match. |
| `"` … `"` | Value quotes | Wrap every comparison value; empty for the two emptiness tests. |
| `\"` `\\` | Escapes | A quote or a backslash inside a value. |
| `]=>` | Replacement marker | Ends the conditions and starts the replacement or text. |
| `if` | Conditional text keyword | Takes the target's place, so the expression writes text. |
| `\|` | Else separator | After the text, starts the text written when the conditions do not match. |

The three shapes are:

```text
[[target][conditions]]
[[target][conditions]=>"replacement"]]
[[if][conditions]=>"text"|"otherwise"]]
```

The first brackets the target, the second replaces it, and the third writes
text. Each ends in `]]`; `|"otherwise"` is optional. `~=""` is not valid.

## Text types and their formats

In a document template, `Text type` and `Format` decide how a value looks. A
tabular `Single field` column uses the same formats through `Value format`.

| Text type | Format options |
|---|---|
| `Normal Text` | `Normal`, `Uppercase`, `Lowercase`, `Capitalize` |
| `Picture` | Sized with width and height rather than a format |
| `Markdown` | `Normal`; renders rich text such as a narrative |
| `Coordinates` | `Decimal`, `Cardinal Dec`, `DMS`, `DDM` |
| `List Values` | Pipe, comma, semicolon, slash, new line, bulleted, custom separator |
| `Nested List` | `Table` or `Card list`; a table adds a `Header Case` control |
| `Dates` | `YYYY-MM-DD`, `DD-MM-YYYY`, `MM-DD-YYYY`, `DD/MM/YYYY`, `MM/DD/YYYY`, `Month DD, YYYY`, `DD Month YYYY`, `DD Mon YYYY` |
| `Date and Time` | Seventeen combinations of the date formats above with 24-hour, 12-hour, ISO, and time-only output |
| `Time` | `24h`, `24h seconds`, `12h`, `12h padded` |
| `Sex` | `Presentation` and `Missing`, described below |
| `Number` | `Original`, or 0, 1, 2, or 3 decimal places |
| `Encoded Text` | `Default mapping` or `Custom mapping...` |

`Nested List` is for related records, such as specimen parts, personnel, or
collecting methods, and is the text type that most often needs dynamic sizing.

### Encoded Text: what decodes

Some fields store a number that stands for a word. `Encoded Text` writes the
word. `Default mapping` uses this table, matched on the end of the field key, so
both `mammalAttribute::testisPosition` and `testisPosition` decode.

| Field key ends with | Code 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `::sex` | Male | Female | Unknown | Gynandromorph | Hermaphrodite | Female? | Male? |
| `::iDConfidence` | Low | Medium | High | | | | |
| `mammalAttribute::age` | Adult | Subadult | Juvenile | Unknown | | | |
| `herpAttribute::age` | Adult | Juvenile | Neonate | Metamorph | Unknown | | |
| `::testisPosition` | Scrotal | Abdominal | | | | | |
| `::epididymisAppearance` | Tubular | Partial | Not Tubular | | | | |
| `::vaginaOpening` | Imperforate | Perforate | | | | | |
| `::pubicSymphysis` | Close | Small Open | Open | | | | |
| `::reproductiveStage` | Nulliparous | Primiparous | Multiparous | | | | |
| `::mammaeCondition` | Small | Large | Lactating | | | | |
| `::echolocation` | FM | CF | QCF | None | | | |
| `::ovaryAppearance` | Smooth | Ova minute | At least one ovum ≥ 1 mm dia. | | | | |
| `::oviductAppearance` | Straight | Convoluted | | | | | |
| `::fat` | None | Trace | Light | Moderate | Heavy | Extremely Heavy | |
| `::bodyMolt` | None | Trace | Light | Moderate | Heavy | | |

These fields decode as `Yes` for `1` and `No` for `0`: `::broodPatch`,
`::hasBursa`, `::wingIsMolt`, `::tailIsMolt`, `::showBatFields`, and
`::showEchoFields`.

Anything else is written unchanged: a field not in the table, a value that is
not a whole number, or a code outside its list. A bare number on a label
usually means one of these. Use `Custom mapping...` to label a field the table
does not cover.

For sex, the `Sex` text type can also write a symbol or letter, and
`[…sex]-img` draws an icon. Use `Encoded Text` on sex only for the plain word.

### Decode an encoded field

The placeholder stays the same; the text type or `#label` turns the code into
its label.

**In a document template,** put the placeholder in a text element and set
`Text type` to `Encoded Text`:

```text
Testes: [mammalAttribute::testisPosition]
```

A specimen that stores `0` prints `Testes: Scrotal`. Each placeholder in the
element decodes against its own field, so one element can hold several:

```text
[mammalAttribute::age], [mammalAttribute::testisPosition]
```

**In a tabular export preset,** use a `Single field` column and set
`Value format` to `Encoded text`. In a `Custom field` column, add `#label` to
the field, or select `Show label` on its segment:

```text
[mammalAttribute::testisPosition#label], [mammalAttribute::mammaeCondition#label]
```

**With `#label`,** one placeholder decodes with the default mapping in any text
type, so an element or column can mix codes and labels. Write it after the key
and before any fallback: `[mammalAttribute::sex#label??Unknown]`. A value with
no default label is written unchanged.

**For your own labels,** choose `Custom mapping...` as the format in the
template editor, then `Map encoded values` to type a label for each code. The
dialog starts from the default labels. In a tabular preset, write the mapping
in `Advanced` > `Raw format option`:

```text
custom_map:0=Scrotal,1=Abdominal
```

Write each pair as `code=label`, separated by commas. Labels cannot contain a
comma or an equals sign, and a code without a pair is written unchanged. A
custom mapping applies to every placeholder in the element, so give a
custom-mapped field its own text element.

Decoding changes only what is written. Conditions still compare the stored
code, and an empty field uses its `No Content Placeholder`.

### Sex formatting

| Control | Options |
|---|---|
| `Format` | `Symbol (♂/♀)`, `Letter (M/F)`, `Text (Male/Female)` |
| `Missing` | `Unknown`, `N/A`, `None` |

An uncertain determination keeps its question mark in every format: `Female?`,
`F?`, `♀?`, and `Male?`, `M?`, `♂?`.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | What to check |
|---|---|
| A conditional prints as plain text | The expression is mistyped. Look for a missing `]]`, an unquoted value, or `&&` and `\|\|` mixed together. |
| A fallback prints `[table::field]` | `No Content Placeholder` is set to `table::field`. Switch it to `Blank`, `N/A`, `None`, or your own text. |
| A sex icon prints as text | The element must contain only `[…sex]-img`, and the key must end in `.sex`. |
| Record images do not appear | The element must contain only `[media::media]` and use the `Picture` text type. |
| A custom field column has an unexpected header | Set `Column name`. Without it, the header comes from the first field or the text. |
| A coded field prints a number | Use `Encoded Text`, or `Encoded text` on a `Single field` column, or add `#label`. Check that the code is in the default mapping. |
| A condition never matches | Conditions compare stored codes, not labels, and are case-sensitive. A blank field only matches `Is empty`. |
| Conditional text prints nothing | Without a second text after `\|`, nothing is written when the conditions do not match. Check that `if` is lowercase. |

## Template editor options

The options below belong to the document template editor only {% ref id="E" /%}. Tabular export
presets do not use them.

Sizes on the canvas are in millimetres (`mm`). Stroke widths, font sizes, and
padding are in PDF points (`pt`, where 1 pt is 1/72 inch). Colours are stored
as `0xAARRGGBB` values but are always chosen through the colour picker.

### Toolbar

| Control | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Add text` | Places a new text element on the canvas. |
| `Add image` | Places a static image from the device. |
| `Add line` | Places a straight rule. |
| `Add shape` | Places a rectangle, ellipse, circle, triangle, or polygon. |
| `Save template` | Writes the template. Use `Save as...` for a variation. |
| `Undo` / `Redo` | Step through the edit history. |
| `Template border` | Opens the outline panel for the template edge. |
| `Show grid` / `Hide grid` | Toggles the alignment grid. |
| `Enable snap` / `Disable snap` | Toggles snapping to the grid and to other elements. |
| Canvas lock | Freezes panning so a drag never moves the canvas. |
| `Select specimen for text preview` | Chooses the record whose values fill the placeholders while you design. |
| `Template settings` {% ref id="D" /%} | Name, description, side count, and template size. |
| `Preset template` {% ref id="A" /%} | Loads a bundled starting point. |
| `Zoom in` / `Zoom out` | Scales the canvas view only; nothing in the output changes. |
| `1 sided` / `Front` / `Back` | Switches side mode and the side being edited. |
| Mirror | Mirrors a side, for tags folded along an edge. |

### Text element options

#### Content

| Option | Values | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Text | Any text, placeholders, conditionals | The element's content. |
| `Text type` | See the table below | Chooses how the value is formatted. |
| `Format` | Depends on the text type | The specific format within that type. |
| `No Content Placeholder` | Blank, `table::field`, `N/A`, `None`, custom | Writes the `??` fallback. |
| `Conditional output` | Brackets, field, value | Writes a `[[ … ]]` expression. |
| `Find and replace` | — | Edits the text of the element. |
| Replacement rules | Pattern, replacement, match type, case sensitivity | Rewrites values after formatting. |
| `Map encoded values` | Default or custom mapping | Turns stored codes into labels for `Encoded Text`. |

#### Type and style

| Option | Values |
|---|---|
| `Font` | Bundled and installed fonts; an uninstalled font is marked `(not installed)` |
| Font size | Points, with increase and decrease buttons |
| `Bold`, `Italic`, `Underline`, `Strikethrough` | On or off |
| Alignment | `Align left`, `Align center`, `Align right` |
| Case | `Normal`, `Uppercase`, `Lowercase`, `Capitalize` |
| Text colour | Colour picker |

#### Box and placement

| Option | Values | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Position | X and Y in mm | Measured from the template's top-left corner. |
| Max width | mm | Text wraps at this width. Unset means no wrapping. |
| Height | mm | Bounds the text box height. |
| Padding | pt | Space inside the box, around the text. |
| Rotation | 0–359 degrees | Whole degrees. |
| Background colour | Colour or none | — |
| Border colour, width, stroke | Colour, pt, `solid` / `dashed` / `dotted` / `double` | — |
| Corner radius | pt | Rounds the box. |
| Dynamic sizing | On or off | Lets the box grow with the text. Pair it with a layout block's `Auto-fill page`. |
| Stacking | `Bring to front`, `Bring forward`, `Send backward`, `Send to back` | — |
| Lock | On or off | A locked element cannot be dragged or resized. |
| Visible | On or off | A hidden element is kept in the template but not printed. |

#### QR code

Turn a text element into a QR code to carry an identifier that a phone can
read back.

| Option | Values |
|---|---|
| QR code | On or off |
| Size | mm |
| Background colour | Colour picker |
| Shape | `Square` or `Circle` |

The QR payload keeps the raw formatted value, including a trailing `.0` that
printed text would drop, so a scanned code matches the stored value exactly.

#### Picture

| Option | Values |
|---|---|
| Width, height | mm |

Only a bare `[media::media]` or `[media]` element resolves to record images.
Mixing the placeholder with other text deliberately does not.

### Image, line, and shape options

Static images, lines, and shapes are decoration. They carry no record data, so
they have no text type, format, or conditional options.

| Element | Options |
|---|---|
| Image | Image path, position, width, height, rotation, stacking, lock, visibility |
| Line | Position, length, rotation, thickness (pt), colour, stroke style, stacking, lock, visibility |
| Shape | Shape type, position, width, height, rotation, stroke thickness, stroke colour, stroke style, fill colour or `Clear fill`, stacking, lock, visibility |

Shape types are `rect`, `ellipse`, `circle`, `triangle`, and `polygon`; a
polygon also takes a side count. Stroke styles for lines and shapes are
`solid`, `dashed`, `dotted`, and `double`.

An image element stores a path, not the picture. A template exported to
another device names the image and asks for it again there.

### Template options

| Option | Values | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `Template name` | Text | Must be unique. Renaming repoints every layout that used the old name. |
| `Description` | Text, up to 80 characters | Optional. |
| `Record type` | `Specimen`, `Specimen Part`, `Site`, `Collecting Event`, `Narrative`, `None` | Controls which fields and records are available. |
| Side count | `1 sided` or `2 sided` | A one-sided template keeps its Back design but does not print it. |
| Width, height | mm | The size of one block, not the sheet. |
| Template border | Style, width (pt), colour | Styles are `solid`, `dashed`, `dotted`, and `double line`. |

Print options saved with a template cover duplex, front and back mirroring,
page size, rows and columns per page, page padding, and document padding.
A print layout preset overrides these for the sheet it produces; see
[Export documents](../export-documents#create-a-print-layout-preset).
